If it's about semantically equivalent code, here are some general rules that might help:

1. Comments and formatting do not affect the meaning of a program. They may change how easily the code is readable or understandable, but they don't change what the code does. 
2. Whitespace (spaces, newlines) can be changed without affecting the functionality of the code.
3. Variables and function/method names do not affect the meaning of a program. They are labels for parts of the program that help humans understand it. Changing them might make sense to you but may not to someone else reading your code. 
4. Operators (+, -, *, /) have the same precedence and associativity in all contexts. This means they perform the operation from left to right without regard for operator precedence rules of mathematics.
5. The order of operations is the same in all contexts. That's what parentheses are for. 
6. Functions or methods do not affect each other, so you can rearrange them as much as you like without affecting the meaning of your program.
7. Constants (like numbers and strings) don't change value; they just have different names. So if a number is used in two places with different names, it doesn't matter which name you use when you refer to it: both will be referring to the same constant. 
8. Control flow (if statements, for loops, while loops) does not affect semantics; only the order of operations within each control structure matters. So if two pieces of code have equivalent control structures, they are semantically equivalent regardless of what happens inside them.
9. Semicolons at the end of lines do not change meaning. They're just syntax that tells the compiler where one operation ends and another begins. 
10. The order in which statements are written does matter; a program is a sequence of operations to be performed, and each statement must be executed in order from top to bottom.

