### Response:
To compare two commits or branches, you can use the `git diff` command with a syntax similar to what you've tried. The correct usage is as follows:

```bash
git diff <commit1>..<commit2>
```

In your case, if you want to see changes between the latest commit on 'master' and your current working directory (HEAD), you can use this command:

```bash
git diff master...HEAD
```

This command compares the two specified commits. The `..` syntax is used for creating a range that includes all the commits from one side of the three-dot to the other, but excludes the tip commit. This means it will show you changes between the latest commit on 'master' and your current HEAD (HEAD points to the last commit in your current branch).

If you want to see a summary of these changes rather than the full diff output, you can use the `--stat` option:

```bash
git diff --stat master...HEAD
```

This will give you a concise summary showing how many files were changed and the number of lines added/removed in each file. This is particularly useful if you're only interested in a high-level overview of changes without getting into the details of individual commits.

