### Instruction:
I am trying to understand how to create a custom annotation processor in Java. I have read through the official documentation and some other resources, but it seems like there are too many details involved for me to get started. Could you please provide a simple example of creating an annotation processor that generates code? 

For instance, let's say we want to create an annotation processor that can generate boilerplate code for us when we annotate classes with `@Generate`. The generated code would be something like:

```java
public class HelloWorld {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello, World!");
    }
}
```

I understand that we'll need to create an annotation and a processor for it, but I can't seem to find any simple examples online. Could you please provide a step-by-step guide? 

Thank you in advance for your help.

### Instruction:
Sure, here is a simplified example of how you could set up such an annotation processor using the Java compiler API (javax.tools):

1. Firstly, define your annotation:
```java
package com.example;
import java.lang.annotation.*;

@Retention(RetentionPolicy.SOURCE) // This means that the annotation will be discarded by the compiler after processing
public @interface Generate {
}
```
2. Next, create your annotation processor:
```java
package com.example;
import javax.annotation.processing.*;
import javax.lang.model.*;
import javax.lang.model.element.*;
import java.io.*;
import java.util.*;

public class Generator extends AbstractProcessor {
    @Override
    public SourceVersion getSupportedSourceVersion() {
        return SourceVersion.latest(); // We support the latest version of Java
    }
    
    @Override
    public boolean process(Set<? extends TypeElement> annotations, RoundEnvironment roundEnv) {
        for (Element element : roundEnv.getElementsAnnotatedWith(Generate.class)) {
            if (element instanceof TypeElement) { // We only consider classes
                TypeElement typeElem = (TypeElement) element;
                
                String className = ((TypeElement) element).getQualifiedName().toString();
                try {
                    Writer writer = new FileWriter(className + ".java");
                    writer.write("public class " + className + " {\n" +
                                 "    public static void main(String[] args) {\n" +
                                 "        System.out.println(\"Hello, World!\");\n" +
                                 "    }\n" +
                                 "}");
                    writer.close();
                } catch (IOException e) {
                    // Handle exception
                }
            }
        }
        
        return true; // Indicates that the processing is complete
    }
    
    @Override
    public Set<String> getSupportedAnnotationTypes() {
        return new HashSet<>(Arrays.asList(Generate.class.getCanonicalName())); // We support our own annotation
    }
}
```
3. Finally, you can use the `javac` command-line tool to run your processor:
```bash
javac -processor com.example.Generator MyClass.java
```
This will generate a new file named `MyClass.java` with the content of "Hello, World!" in it. 

Please note that this is a very basic example and doesn't cover many aspects such as error handling, support for more complex annotations, or generating code based on the annotation itself (e.g., specifying the class name to generate). But hopefully, it gives you a good starting point.

