A: The `git difftool` command is used for comparing the changes in your working tree with the index or a commit, but it doesn't work directly with Beyond Compare as you have configured it to use. Instead, it uses whatever merge tool has been set up by default on your system (which may not be Beyond Compare).

To compare two branches using Beyond Compare as your difftool:
1. Open Git Bash terminal.
2. Navigate to the directory of your git project.
3. Run `git difftool branchname`, replacing "branchname" with the name of the branch you want to compare against. 

If it doesn't work, there might be a problem with setting up Beyond Compare as Git's diff tool or perhaps the path to Beyond Compare is not set correctly in your system environment variables.

To setup Beyond Compare as git difftool:
1. Open Beyond Compare and go to `Edit > Preferences`.
2. In the preferences window, navigate to `Miscellaneous > Diff Tools`.
3. Click on `Add...` button.
4. A new dialog will open. Here you need to provide a title for your diff tool (like "Beyond Compare"), and in the Program field, enter the full path of Beyond Compare executable file (you can find it by typing `bcompare` in Git Bash). In Args field, you should put something like this: `"$LOCAL" "$REMOTE"`.
5. Click OK to close all dialogs.
6. Now go back to the terminal and try running `git difftool branchname` again. It should work now. 

If it still doesn't, you might need to provide more details about your setup or check if Beyond Compare is correctly installed on your system.

