### Response:
Semantic equivalence in software engineering usually refers to the fact that two pieces of code do essentially the same thing, but may have different syntax or structure due to various factors like coding style, comments, etc.

To detect semantic equivalence between two source code files, you could use a diff tool such as `diff` (Unix/Linux) or `WinMerge` (Windows). These tools can compare the content of text-based files and highlight differences in syntax. However, they might not be able to accurately determine if two pieces of code are semantically equivalent because they only consider syntactic differences.

For more accurate semantic equivalence detection, you would need a tool that understands the semantics of programming languages. For Java for instance, tools like `PMD` (Programming Mistake Detector) or `CheckStyle` can analyze your code and report potential issues based on coding standards. 

Another approach is to use static analysis tools such as `SonarQube` which perform a wide range of checks including semantic checks. They can detect things like possible bugs, suspicious constructs, code smells etc.

For example, if you have two Java files:

File1:
```java
public class Test {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        System.out.println("Hello World");
    }
}
```

File2:
```java
import java.util.Scanner;

public class HelloWorld {
   public static void main(String[] args){
      Scanner scan = new Scanner(System.in);
      System.out.print("Enter your name: ");
      String name = scan.nextLine();
      System.out.println("Hello, " + name);
   }
}
```

If you use a diff tool on these two files, it will show that they are different in terms of syntax but semantically equivalent because both print out a greeting message to the console. 

However, if you run static analysis tools like SonarQube, it would report potential issues for File2 as it uses `System.out` instead of `System.console()` which is considered a bad practice in Java.

So, while diff and other simple text-based comparison tools can help detect syntactic differences, they are not enough to determine semantic equivalence. For more accurate results, you would need to use specialized static analysis or code reviewing tools.

