I am trying to understand how semantic equivalence works in software engineering and how it is applied in practice. 

Semantic equivalence refers to the situation where two pieces of code are functionally equivalent, meaning they produce the same output for the same input. This concept is important because it helps developers ensure that changes made to one piece of code do not unintentionally break another piece of code. It also allows for easier refactoring and maintenance of software systems.

In terms of tools, semantic equivalence can be checked using static analysis tools or by running tests on the system under test (SUT). These tools typically use some form of abstract syntax tree comparison to determine if two pieces of code are semantically equivalent. 

However, determining semantic equivalence is not always straightforward and often requires a deep understanding of both the source code and its intended functionality. It's also worth noting that even when two pieces of code are semantically equivalent, they may behave differently in different contexts or with different inputs due to differences in execution environment, configuration settings, etc.

In conclusion, semantic equivalence is an important concept in software engineering for ensuring the stability and correctness of systems. It's a tool that developers can use to help ensure code quality and maintainability.

