### Instruction:
I'm trying to understand if there is a way to use the `git diff` command to compare two branches without having to checkout one branch or switch to it first?

### Response:
Yes, you can do that by using the `git diff` command with two commit hashes. Here are the steps:

1. First, find out the commit hash of the last common ancestor (MCA) between your branches. You can use this command to get it: 
   ```bash
   git merge-base branch1 branch2
   ```
   Replace `branch1` and `branch2` with the names or hashes of your two branches. This will print out a commit hash that is common to both branches.

2. Then, you can compare these two branches using:
   ```bash
   git diff <commit-hash> branch1..branch2
   ```
   Replace `<commit-hash>` with the MCA commit hash obtained in step 1. This command will show all changes from the point of the common ancestor to the tip of `branch2`, but not those made on `branch1`.

This way you can compare two branches without having to checkout or switch to one branch first.

