Sure, let me elaborate on that. Semantic equivalence is essentially about comparing two pieces of code to determine if they have the same meaning or functionality. It doesn't necessarily mean syntactically identical; rather, it means they behave in exactly the same way for all possible inputs. 

For instance, consider two methods: one that sorts an array using bubble sort and another that uses quick sort. They are semantically equivalent because both will correctly sort any given array of comparable elements regardless of their implementation details (i.e., how they perform the sort). 

